id: ops-release-generator-timezone
title: Align tagged changelog headings with the configured release timezone
description: "Follow-up from Round AX on ops-release-1115-heading-drift. Installed pm-changelog 2026.11.6 and the continuation guard date tagged headings from the literal committer-date prefix. Before Round CM, preparation used the RELEASE_TIMEZONE day for its heading pin. A synthetic commit at 2026-11-06T23:30:00Z resolves to 2026-11-07 in Europe/Vienna, but resolveReleaseContext for a tag on that commit returns 2026-11-06 even when dateFallback is 2026-11-07. The preparation pin must use the expected squash commit day while the release timezone still governs scheduling and the daily cap."
type: Issue
status: in_progress
priority: 2
tags[2]: "area:release","gh:unbraind/pm-ops#295"
created_at: "2026-11-17T15:02:27.928Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-18T16:40:39.293Z"
assignee: codex-sol
claim_principal: codex-sol
author: codex-sol
acceptance_criteria: A real tagged-commit regression at a UTC/configured-timezone day boundary proves the prepared heading and post-tag regeneration agree; retain stable historical tag dates and never gate already accepted artifacts on heading validation.
comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-17T16:21:39.329Z",codex-sol,"Round AZ: the continuation heading check now mirrors the pinned pm-changelog generator instead of converting through RELEASE_TIMEZONE. It reads the tagged commit timestamp with git log -1 --format=%cI HEAD and compares the literal YYYY-MM-DD prefix with --date-fallback. Generator evidence: node_modules/pm-changelog/dist/release-context.js:153,496,554-558 in pm-changelog 2026.11.6. Real tagged regressions show 2026-11-06T23:30:00Z expects 2026-11-06 and the same instant at +01:00 expects 2026-11-07. Preparation coordination remains a separate concern; this item stays open."
  "2026-11-18T16:30:17.874Z",codex-sol,"Round CM verified 16 unresolved findings on PR #295 at 2e59f07. Checking the tagged committer day, canonical hook parsing, and candidate fixture fidelity before running the release gates."
